### TL;DR  
MapLibre (and Mapbox GL JS) can only use **one sprite sheet per style**.  
If the `sprite` URL in the style points at the basemap provider, every `icon‑image` lookup will be resolved against that sheet – there is no way to tell a single layer “look at a different sprite”.  

You have three practical ways to get your own POI icons on the map without rebuilding the provider’s sprite:

| Option | What you do | Pros | Cons |
|--------|-------------|------|------|
| **1️⃣ Merge the two sprite sheets** (or create a new combined one) | Use a tool like **`spritezero`**, **`mapbox-gl-sprite`**, or any image‑packer to combine the provider’s PNG/JSON with yours, then point the style’s `sprite` URL at the new file. | One‑sprite‑sheet solution – works exactly like the original basemap icons. | Requires a build step and you have to keep the combined sprite in sync when the provider updates. |
| **2️⃣ Load your icons with `map.addImage` / `map.loadImage`** | Load each POI PNG (or SVG) at runtime and register it with the map instance. Then reference the name you gave it in `icon-image`. | No need to touch the original style or sprite; you can keep your icons on a completely separate domain. | Slightly more JavaScript, each image is a separate HTTP request (unless you pre‑bundle them yourself). |
| **3️⃣ Use a second style / separate map instance for your POIs** | Create a tiny “overlay” style that only contains your POI layer(s) and points its `sprite` at your sheet, then add it as a **style layer** on top of the basemap (via `map.addSource` + `map.addLayer`). | Keeps the two sprite sheets completely independent. | You now have two style objects to maintain; the overlay style can’t reuse the basemap’s layers. |

Below you’ll find the **step‑by‑step code** for the two approaches that don’t require rebuilding the provider’s sprite (options 2 and 3). Choose the one that best fits your workflow.

---

## 1️⃣ Why the “single sprite” rule exists

The GL style spec defines a **global `sprite` property**:

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sprite": "https://basemap.provider.com/sprites/sprite",
  ...
}
```

All `icon-image` values are resolved against the **single** sprite sheet referenced there. The spec does **not** have a per‑layer `sprite` field, nor an expression that can switch between sprite URLs. Therefore the engine never looks anywhere else, which is why your custom names silently resolve to “nothing” (the engine just draws a transparent 0×0 image).

Because of that limitation you have to either:

* make the wanted icons part of the *same* sprite sheet, **or**
* bypass the sprite mechanism entirely and add the images manually.

---

## 2️⃣ Loading your own icons with `map.addImage`

### 2.1. Basic pattern (PNG, JPEG, SVG)

```js
// Assuming you already have a MapLibre GL JS map instance:
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://basemap.provider.com/style.json',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});

// List of POI icons you want to use (name → URL)
const myIcons = {
  'cafe':   'https://mydomain.com/icons/cafe.png',
  'library':'https://mydomain.com/icons/library.svg',
  // add as many as you need
};

map.on('load', () => {
  // Load each image and register it with the map
  const promises = Object.entries(myIcons).map(([name, url]) => {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      // `loadImage` works for PNG/JPEG/SVG (SVG needs a recent browser)
      map.loadImage(url, (error, image) => {
        if (error) return reject(error);
        // `addImage` registers the image under the given name.
        // `true` = make it SDF (optional, only if you need color tinting)
        map.addImage(name, image, { sdf: false });
        resolve();
      });
    });
  });

  Promise.all(promises)
    .then(() => {
      // Now you can use the names in your style layers:
      map.addLayer({
        id: 'my-pois',
        type: 'symbol',
        source: {
          type: 'geojson',
          data: 'https://mydomain.com/pois.geojson'
        },
        layout: {
          'icon-image': ['get', 'icon'],   // expects a property `icon` in each feature
          'icon-size': 0.8,
          'icon-allow-overlap': true
        }
      });
    })
    .catch(err => console.error('Failed to load POI icons', err));
});
```

**Key points**

| What | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|
| `map.loadImage(url, callback)` | Asynchronously fetches the image and decodes it into an `ImageBitmap` that the GL engine can use. |
| `map.addImage(name, image, {sdf: false})` | Registers the bitmap under `name`. The `icon-image` property later looks up that name. |
| `icon-image: ['get', 'icon']` | If your GeoJSON features have a property `icon` that matches the name you registered (`cafe`, `library`, …), the correct image is used per feature. |
| `icon-allow-overlap: true` (optional) | Prevents the engine from hiding icons that would otherwise collide. |
| **Cross‑origin** – If your icons are on a different domain, make sure the server sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your origin). Otherwise `loadImage` will fail silently in some browsers. |

### 2.2. Using a single pre‑packed sprite‑like JSON (optional)

If you have dozens of icons and want to avoid many HTTP requests, you can still **pre‑bundle** them into a single PNG + JSON *outside* of the style spec, then load that bundle yourself:

```js
// 1️⃣ Load the sprite PNG
map.loadImage('https://mydomain.com/my-sprite.png', (err, spriteImg) => {
  if (err) throw err;

  // 2️⃣ Load the JSON that maps names → rects
  fetch('https://mydomain.com/my-sprite.json')
    .then(r => r.json())
    .then(spriteJSON => {
      // 3️⃣ For each entry, extract a sub‑image and register it
      Object.entries(spriteJSON).forEach(([name, {x, y, width, height, pixelRatio}]) => {
        // Create a canvas to cut out the sub‑image
        const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
        canvas.width = width;
        canvas.height = height;
        const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
        ctx.drawImage(
          spriteImg,
          x * pixelRatio, y * pixelRatio, width * pixelRatio, height * pixelRatio,
          0, 0, width, height
        );
        const imageData = ctx.getImageData(0, 0, width, height);
        map.addImage(name, imageData);
      });

      // Now add your POI layer as shown above
    });
});
```

This mimics the normal sprite workflow but stays completely under your control, and you still only need **one** HTTP request for the PNG and one for the JSON.

---

## 3️⃣ Using a second style (overlay) that points at your own sprite

If you prefer to keep the basemap style untouched **and** you already have a full GL style for your POIs (maybe you exported it from Mapbox Studio), you can **stack two styles** on the same map:

```js
// 1️⃣ Basemap (provider)
const basemap = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://basemap.provider.com/style.json',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});

// 2️⃣ POI overlay – a *separate* map instance that shares the same canvas
const poiOverlay = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',          // same container
  style: 'https://mydomain.com/poi-style.json', // this style has its own `sprite`
  interactive: false,       // we don't need UI on the overlay
  attributionControl: false // avoid duplicate attributions
});

// 3️⃣ Keep the two maps in sync
function sync() {
  const center = basemap.getCenter();
  const zoom = basemap.getZoom();
  const bearing = basemap.getBearing();
  const pitch = basemap.getPitch();

  poiOverlay.jumpTo({center, zoom, bearing, pitch});
}
basemap.on('move', sync);
basemap.on('zoom', sync);
basemap.on('rotate', sync);
basemap.on('pitch', sync);
```

**Why this works**

* Each map instance has its **own** `sprite` URL, so the POI style can point at your custom sprite sheet.
* The overlay’s layers are drawn **after** the basemap’s layers because the second map’s canvas sits on top (or you can use `maplibregl.Map#addLayer` on the first map after the basemap loads, but that would still need a single sprite).

**Caveats**

* You now have **two WebGL contexts** (two map instances) which doubles GPU memory usage. For most browsers this is fine, but on low‑end devices you may see a performance hit.
* Interaction events (click, hover) will only fire on the *top* map (`poiOverlay`). If you need interactivity on the basemap, forward events manually:

```js
poiOverlay.on('click', e => basemap.fire('click', e));
```

---

## 4️⃣ Quick checklist – why your icons were invisible

| ✅ Check | What to verify |
|--------|----------------|
| **Sprite URL** | The style’s `sprite` points to the provider’s sheet, not yours. |
| **Icon name spelling** | The name you set in `icon-image` must match exactly the key in the sprite JSON (or the name you passed to `addImage`). |
| **Cross‑origin** | If you load PNGs from another domain, the server must send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`. |
| **Image size** | Very large icons (e.g., > 1024 px) can be culled if `icon-size` is too small or if the image exceeds the GL texture size limit. |
| **SDF vs non‑SDF** | If you set `sdf: true` when adding the image but the PNG isn’t an SDF, the icon will appear completely transparent. |
| **Layer order** | Your POI layer must be **above** any raster or background layers that could cover it. Use `map.moveLayer('my-pois', 'waterway-label')` or similar. |
| **Feature property** | If you use a data‑driven `icon-image` (`['get', 'icon']`), make sure each feature actually has that property. |
| **Console errors** | Some browsers swallow GL errors; open the **WebGL** debug console (Chrome → `chrome://gpu` → “WebGL errors”) to see if the texture failed to upload. |

If you go through that list and still see nothing, the most common culprit is **cross‑origin** – the image loads, but the browser refuses to use it as a texture, resulting in a silent “transparent” icon.

---

