### TL;DR  
The “`text‑anchor === undefined && symbol‑placement === 'point'`” test is **not a reliable way to spot route‑shield symbol layers**.  
Most plain place‑name layers also use point placement and leave `text‑anchor` unset (it just falls back to the default `"center"`), so they will be flagged incorrectly.  

A more robust heuristic should look at **what the layer is actually drawing**, i.e. the presence of an **icon** that comes from a shield‑style sprite **plus** a **text field** that contains a route number (or a similar pattern). In practice you can combine a few of the following cues:

| Cue | Why it helps | Typical value for shields |
|-----|--------------|---------------------------|
| `layout.icon-image` (or `paint['icon-color']`) | Shields are drawn as icons, not just pure text. | A sprite name that contains “shield”, “road‑shield”, “route‑shield”, etc. |
| `layout.text-field` | Shields usually show the route number on top of the icon. | A string that matches a route‑number regex (`/^[0-9]+[A-Z]?(‑[0-9]+)?$/`). |
| `layout['icon-allow-overlap'] === true` **or** `layout['icon-ignore-placement'] === true` | Shields need to sit on top of the road line without being culled. | Often `true`. |
| `layout['text-allow-overlap'] === true` **or** `layout['text-ignore-placement'] === true` | The route number must be visible even when other labels are nearby. | Often `true`. |
| `layout['icon-size']` (or a constant `icon-size` in `paint`) | Shields are usually scaled to a specific size (e.g. `0.8`‑`1.2`). | Any non‑default size is a hint. |
| `metadata` (if present) | Some style authors add a custom flag (`metadata['shield'] = true`). | Direct and reliable if you control the style. |
| Layer **id** or **source‑layer** naming conventions | Many styles name shield layers `road_shield`, `highway_shield`, `shield‑*`, etc. | Simple string match. |

Below is a **sample JavaScript/TypeScript snippet** you can drop into a MapLibre‑GL style‑audit script. It tries the most common signals first and falls back to a regex on the sprite name if you have a sprite sheet that follows a naming convention.

```ts
/**
 * Returns true if a symbol layer looks like a route‑shield layer.
 */
function isRouteShieldLayer(layer: maplibregl.AnyLayer): boolean {
  // Must be a symbol layer
  if (layer.type !== 'symbol') return false;

  const layout = layer.layout ?? {};
  const paint   = layer.paint   ?? {};

  // 1️⃣  Presence of an icon (the shield graphic)
  const iconImage = layout['icon-image'];
  if (!iconImage) return false; // No icon → not a shield

  // 2️⃣  Does the icon name look like a shield sprite?
  //    (adjust the regex to match your sprite naming scheme)
  const iconLooksLikeShield = typeof iconImage === 'string' &&
    /shield|road[_-]shield|route[_-]shield|sign[_-]shield/i.test(iconImage);
  if (!iconLooksLikeShield) return false;

  // 3️⃣  Presence of a text field (the route number)
  const textField = layout['text-field'];
  if (!textField) return false;

  // 4️⃣  Does the text look like a route number?
  //    This catches “I‑5”, “US‑101”, “A12”, “12‑B”, etc.
  const routeNumberRegex = /^[A-Z]{0,2}\-?\d{1,4}[A-Z]{0,2}$/i;
  const textLooksLikeRoute = typeof textField === 'string' &&
    routeNumberRegex.test(textField);
  if (!textLooksLikeRoute) return false;

  // 5️⃣  Overlap / placement hints (optional but common)
  const allowOverlap = layout['icon-allow-overlap'] === true ||
                       layout['icon-ignore-placement'] === true ||
                       layout['text-allow-overlap'] === true ||
                       layout['text-ignore-placement'] === true;

  // 6️⃣  Symbol placement – shields are usually point‑placed,
  //     but we don’t *require* it because the default is “point”.
  const isPointPlacement = layout['symbol-placement'] === 'point' ||
                           layout['symbol-placement'] === undefined; // default

  // If we got this far, we have a very strong case.
  return allowOverlap && isPointPlacement;
}
```

#### How the heuristic works

| Step | What it checks | Why it reduces false‑positives |
|------|----------------|--------------------------------|
| **1** | `icon-image` exists | Plain place‑name layers usually have **no icon** (they’re pure text). |
| **2** | Sprite name contains “shield” (or similar) | Even if an icon is present, most non‑shield icons are “dot”, “marker”, “city‑dot”, etc. |
| **3** | `text-field` exists | A shield without a route number is rare; a place‑name layer may have a `text-field` but no icon. |
| **4** | Text matches a route‑number pattern | Prevents “Paris”, “New York”, etc., from being flagged. |
| **5** | Overlap flags are true | Shields are deliberately allowed to overlap other labels; most place‑name layers keep the defaults (`false`). |
| **6** | `symbol-placement` is point (or default) | Keeps the check compatible with styles that omit the property (the default is `"point"`). |

You can **tune** the regexes and the sprite‑name pattern to match the conventions used in the particular style you’re auditing.

---

## Why the original heuristic fails

| Condition | What it actually means in the spec | Typical value for a place‑name layer |
|-----------|------------------------------------|--------------------------------------|
| `layout['text-anchor'] === undefined` | The property is **not set**, so the renderer falls back to the default `"center"`. | Most label layers never set `text-anchor`; they rely on the default. |
| `layout['symbol-placement'] === 'point'` | The symbols are placed at a single point (the default). | Again, the default for most point‑based label layers is `"point"`. |

Consequently, **any point‑placed symbol layer that doesn’t explicitly set `text-anchor`** (which is the majority of point‑based label layers) will be flagged, regardless of whether it’s a shield or a city name.

---

## If you can’t rely on sprite names

Some styles use a **single generic sprite** (e.g., `"marker"` or `"dot"`) for all point symbols and then draw the shield shape via `icon-color` + `icon-size`. In that case you can still differentiate by looking at **combined icon + text patterns**:

```ts
// Fallback: if the icon is generic but the text looks like a route number,
// treat it as a shield.
if (iconImage === 'marker' && textLooksLikeRoute) return true;
```

Or, if the style author has added **metadata**:

```ts
if (layer.metadata?.['mapbox:group']?.includes('shield')) return true;
```

---

## Quick checklist for a robust “shield‑detector”

1. **Layer type** = `symbol`.
2. **`icon-image`** is defined **and** its value matches a shield‑like naming pattern **or** the layer’s `metadata` marks it as a shield.
3. **`text-field`** is defined **and** matches a route‑number regex.
4. (Optional but recommended) **Overlap flags** (`icon-allow-overlap`, `icon-ignore-placement`, `text-allow-overlap`, `text-ignore-placement`) are `true`.
5. **`symbol-placement`** is `"point"` **or** omitted (default).
6. **`text-anchor`** is either omitted (default `"center"`) **or** explicitly set to `"center"` – we *don’t* use its presence/absence as a decision factor.

If you satisfy **1 + 2 + 3** you already have a high‑confidence shield detection; adding **4** and **5** reduces the chance of false positives even further.

---

### Bottom line

- **No**, the heuristic based on `text-anchor` being undefined is not sound.
- **Yes**, you should key off the **presence of an icon that looks like a shield** *and* a **text field that looks like a route number**, optionally reinforced by overlap/placement flags or explicit metadata.

Implement the snippet (or a variant that matches your sprite naming conventions) and you should see the false‑positives on plain place‑name layers disappear while real shield layers get correctly flagged. Happy auditing!

Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste ready** example that shows how to

1. **Create a MapLibre GL JS map**  
2. **Add a draggable marker (pin) at a given longitude/latitude**  
3. **Show a popup when the user clicks the pin**  
4. **Update the popup (or any UI) when the pin is dragged**  

You can drop the code into a plain HTML file and open it in a browser (no build tools required).

---

## 1️⃣  HTML skeleton

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre – Draggable Pin + Popup</title>

  <!-- MapLibre GL JS CSS -->
  <link
    href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css"
    rel="stylesheet"
  />

  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }

    /* Optional: make the default marker a little bigger */
    .maplibregl-marker {
      cursor: grab;
    }
    .maplibregl-marker:active {
      cursor: grabbing;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<div id="map"></div>

<!-- MapLibre GL JS library -->
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

<script>
  // All the JavaScript lives here – see the next section
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

> **Why the CDN?**  
> The `unpkg.com` URLs point to the latest stable 4.x release of MapLibre GL JS at the time of writing. If you prefer a specific version, replace `4.2.2` with the version you need.

---

## 2️⃣  JavaScript – create the map, the marker, and the popup

```js
// ---------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Initialise the map
// ---------------------------------------------------
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',                     // id of the div above
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any valid style URL
  center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],     // initial centre [lng, lat] (San Francisco)
  zoom: 12
});

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Create a popup that we will reuse
// ---------------------------------------------------
const popup = new maplibregl.Popup({
  closeButton: false,
  closeOnClick: false,
  offset: 25               // distance from the marker tip
});

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣  Create a **draggable** marker (the “pin”)
// ---------------------------------------------------
const marker = new maplibregl.Marker({
  draggable: true,         // <‑‑ makes it draggable
  color: '#ff0000'         // optional: change the pin colour
})
  .setLngLat([-122.447303, 37.753574]) // start position (same as map centre)
  .addTo(map);

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 4️⃣  Show the popup when the user clicks the marker
// ---------------------------------------------------
marker.getElement().addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  // Prevent the map click handler from also firing
  e.stopPropagation();

  // Set the popup content – you can put any HTML you like here
  const lngLat = marker.getLngLat();
  popup
    .setHTML(
      `<strong>Pin location</strong><br>
       lng: ${lngLat.lng.toFixed(5)}<br>
       lat: ${lngLat.lat.toFixed(5)}`
    )
    .setLngLat(lngLat)   // anchor the popup to the marker
    .addTo(map);
});

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 5️⃣  (Optional) Update the popup when the marker is dragged
// ---------------------------------------------------
marker.on('dragend', () => {
  const lngLat = marker.getLngLat();

  // If the popup is already open, update its position & content
  if (popup.isOpen()) {
    popup
      .setHTML(
        `<strong>Pin location (after drag)</strong><br>
         lng: ${lngLat.lng.toFixed(5)}<br>
         lat: ${lngLat.lat.toFixed(5)}`
      )
      .setLngLat(lngLat);
  }

  // You could also do something else here, e.g. send the new coords to a server
  console.log('Marker dragged to:', lngLat);
});

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 6️⃣  Close the popup when clicking anywhere else on the map
// ---------------------------------------------------
map.on('click', () => {
  popup.remove();
});
```

### What each block does

| Block | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| **Map init** | Creates the map, sets a style, centre, and zoom. |
| **Popup** | Instantiates a reusable `Popup` object (no close button, no auto‑close on map click). |
| **Marker** | Creates a red, draggable marker and places it at the start coordinates. |
| **Click listener** | When the user clicks the marker, the popup is populated with the current coordinates and shown. |
| **dragend listener** | Fires after the user finishes dragging. It updates the popup (if open) and logs the new location. |
| **Map click listener** | Clicking anywhere else on the map closes the popup, mimicking the default Mapbox/MapLibre behaviour. |

---

## 3️⃣  Tweaking & Extending

| Want to… | How to do it |
|----------|--------------|
| **Use a custom icon** | Replace the default marker with an HTML element or an image: <br>`new maplibregl.Marker({ element: myCustomDiv, draggable: true })` |
| **Show the popup automatically on load** | Call `marker.togglePopup()` after adding the marker, or just `popup.addTo(map)` with the same `lngLat`. |
| **Persist the new coordinates** | Inside the `dragend` handler, send `lngLat` to your backend via `fetch`/`axios`. |
| **Limit dragging to a bounding box** | In the `drag` event (fires continuously while dragging) you can clamp `lngLat` to a rectangle and call `marker.setLngLat(clampedLngLat)`. |
| **Add a tooltip that follows the cursor** | Use `new maplibregl.Popup({ closeButton: false, closeOnClick: false })` and attach it to `marker.getElement().addEventListener('mouseenter', …)` and `mouseleave`. |
| **Multiple draggable pins** | Create an array of markers, each with its own `dragend` listener. You can store the marker objects in a `Map` keyed by an ID. |

---

## 4️⃣  Full file (copy‑paste)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre – Draggable Pin + Popup</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
    .maplibregl-marker { cursor: grab; }
    .maplibregl-marker:active { cursor: grabbing; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  // ---- Initialise the map -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12
  });

  // ---- Re‑usable popup ----------------------------------------------------
  const popup = new maplibregl.Popup({ closeButton:false, closeOnClick:false, offset:25 });

  // ---- Draggable marker ----------------------------------------------------
  const marker = new maplibregl.Marker({ draggable:true, color:'#ff0000' })
    .setLngLat([-122.447303, 37.753574])
    .addTo(map);

  // ---- Show popup on click -------------------------------------------------
  marker.getElement().addEventListener('click', (e) => {
    e.stopPropagation();               // don't trigger map click
    const lngLat = marker.getLngLat();
    popup
      .setHTML(
        `<strong>Pin location</strong><br>
         lng: ${lngLat.lng.toFixed(5)}<br>
         lat: ${lngLat.lat.toFixed(5)}`
      )
      .setLngLat(lngLat)
      .addTo(map);
  });

  // ---- Update popup after drag --------------------------------------------
  marker.on('dragend', () => {
    const lngLat = marker.getLngLat();
    if (popup.isOpen()) {
      popup
        .setHTML(
          `<strong>Pin location (after drag)</strong><br>
           lng: ${lngLat.lng.toFixed(5)}<br>
           lat: ${lngLat.lat.toFixed(5)}`
        )
        .setLngLat(lngLat);
    }
    console.log('Marker dragged to:', lngLat);
  });

  // ---- Close popup when clicking elsewhere ---------------------------------
  map.on('click', () => popup.remove());
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open that file in a browser, drag the red pin around, and click it to see the popup with the current coordinates. 🎉

